# AutoStash Planning and Execution

Status: Phases 1–5 complete; Phase 6 remains planned.
Source proposal: [PlanningAndExecutionProposal.md](PlanningAndExecutionProposal.md).
Purpose: Extract shared incremental planning and engine execution while preserving the established functional contract.

## Scope and execution rules

The complete source proposal governs every task below, including its Established regression contract, Data contracts, Policy contracts, Incremental planning and execution, Client assessment, Target lifecycle and partial failure, Source layout and dependencies, Validation requirements and Evidence limits. Each phase names the sections most directly applicable; those names do not discard inherited requirements. Read affected current source and regression fixtures before each extraction.

Keep engine inventory/slot objects, GetBestSuitedSlot and TryPutInto authoritative. Do not implement a parallel suitability algorithm, simulated inventories, direct stack-size mutation, precomputed exact move lists, rollback, asynchronous work, client-authored instructions or cross-tick/persisted plans. Alloy deposit execution and general inventory frameworks remain outside scope.

Preserve source/slot order, item-type snapshots, stack compatibility, bloomery required slots/live limits, client-only gates, attached capacity uncertainty, session ownership, persisted partial progress, exception identity, dirty effects, audits and owner-only direct-failure chat. Preserve interaction timing, progress ownership through IProgressSystemProvider, feedback, help, packet formats, permissions and engine behavior installation/Harmony hooks. No policy or gameplay change is implied by extraction.

Use directories Planning, Transfers and Targets under VanillaExpanded/src/AutoStashing, with one named responsibility per file. Keep public composition roots thin and dependencies directional. Interfaces require distinct implementations or a meaningful boundary. Add XML documentation to classes/methods, explain nontrivial logic and group methods by visibility/function. Use Moq for observations and existing real engine fixtures for functional behavior.

All builds/tests run through subagents. After each phase run the focused AutoStash suite and repository Unit suite; run the separate integration suite whenever entry wiring changes and at final acceptance. Integration installs actual production patches only in its dedicated host, removes ordinary owners in finally, and releases the reverse stand-in through host termination before fresh unit hosts. Never start a game, client or server; live acceptance remains user-run and separate.

Review each change twice, including linked-document requirements and assertion sensitivity, and obtain an independent completion audit before checking markers. Keep concise commands/results/decisions in this plan or working context; do not create separate audit, verification or traceability files. A failing regression blocks completion until resolved explicitly; do not weaken, skip or exclude it to obtain green counts.

## Starting evidence and decisions

Coverage prerequisite completed before this plan. Historical final evidence at Git revision 476f3b3, docs/auto-stash/FunctionalTestCoverageEvidence.md: 357 AutoStash, 553 Unit and 1 isolated integration test passed, zero failures/skips/exclusions. The completed documents were intentionally removed in 3917cfa; do not recreate them. Refresh these results against the selected implementation revision before extracting code. Installed engine versions in that evidence were API/Lib/SurvivalMod/Essentials assembly 1.22.7.0, API file 1.22.0 and the others 1.22.7; verify current metadata rather than assuming it.

The existing retry, failure-finalization and progress-provider fixes are part of the preservation baseline. Generic crate-policy fixtures do not establish specialized crate restrictions; use the initialized crate fixtures as well. Both attached-bag paths require independently reloaded persisted bag and owner state, not only wrapper contents.

The unused optional preferred-slot argument has exclusive valid-index preflight but execution fallback after progress. Before migrating its tests, choose and document a compatibility representation preserving that characterization, or obtain an explicit separate decision to remove/change it. Required-slot bloomery routing must never inherit preferred-slot fallback. This is an implementation decision, not authorization to normalize semantics silently.

Exact internal type/interface signatures are chosen during implementation within the proposal responsibilities. Transfer-result contracts must preserve engine-required priority, and operation results must separately retain exhausted direct attempts alongside positive movement. Mutation progress belongs to the service; visual progress remains in interaction owners.

## Phase 1: Establish concrete engine transfer contracts

Outcome: Both existing transfer loops share an immediate engine executor without changing their planning or lifecycle.
Dependencies: Completed coverage prerequisite.
Sources: Proposal Data contracts, Incremental planning and execution, Source layout and dependencies; current AutoStashTransferService, BlockBehaviorAutoStashable and AutoStashMutationState.

☑ P1.1 Refresh the separate Integration, focused AutoStash and Unit baselines through subagents; record revision, exact commands, versions, counts, warnings and host isolation in this plan.
☑ P1.2 Add Transfers/InventoryTransfer and InventoryTransferResult: concrete engine source/destination slots, positive requested quantity, existing move settings, actual movement and RequiredPriority. Keep instructions local to immediate server execution and invalid-instruction checks separate from target policy.
☑ P1.3 Add InventoryTransferExecutor delegating to the engine mutation API. Preserve each path's existing overload/operation semantics, restrictions and callbacks; inspect the bloomery convenience overload before replacing it with an explicit operation.
☑ P1.4 Route shared-container and bloomery mutation calls through the executor while retaining their existing selection, ordering, audit and lifecycle loops. Begin mutation uncertainty before the executor call and clear it only on normal return; exceptions continue to unwind unchanged.
☑ P1.5 Add only boundary tests needed for new contract validity, settings/priority reporting and exception propagation; reuse full-operation regressions for quantities, restrictions and callback mutation.
☑ Completion: Both loops use the executor, focused/Unit suites pass, and second review plus independent audit confirm actual engine behavior/settings and uncertain mutation remain unchanged.

### Implementation notes and evidence

P1.1 baseline refreshed before edits at cb58a0278563157353efb2af56af082b1ed9c0e7 through baseline_tests. These commands ran sequentially in separate hosts, each exiting before the next:

```powershell
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests/VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests.csproj --no-restore --filter Category=Integration --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-build --no-restore --filter FullyQualifiedName~AutoStash --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-build --no-restore --filter Category=Unit --verbosity minimal
```

Baseline: Integration 1, AutoStash 357 and Unit 553 passed; zero failures/skips. Fresh unit hosts followed integration-host termination, isolating its reverse-patched stand-in. Initial sandbox output-write denial was recovered through approved escalation. Existing nullable warnings CS8600/CS8602/CS8603/CS8604 and unused-field warning CS0414 were observed; production emitted seven warning occurrences, while truncated test-build output does not establish an exact combined warning count. No game/client/server process was started.

Current installed metadata independently read from G:/Vintagestory: API, Lib, SurvivalMod and Essentials assembly versions all 1.22.7.0; API file version 1.22.0, others 1.22.7.

P1.2–P1.4 -> proposal Data contracts, Incremental planning and execution, Target lifecycle and partial failure, Source layout and dependencies -> immutable concrete instruction, actual result, original virtual overloads/settings, unchanged engine authority and mutation uncertainty -> Transfers/InventoryTransfer, InventoryTransferResult, InventoryTransferInvocation and InventoryTransferExecutor; only mutation calls/result consumption changed in existing shared-container and bloomery loops. Mutation tracking, selection, retry ordering, audits and finalization remain in their original owners.

Installed API IL confirms bloomery's convenience overload constructs Left/None/AutoMerge and calls the virtual ref-operation overload. The convenience method itself is also virtual, with real engine overrides elsewhere. EngineDefaults instructions therefore retain that exact overload rather than normalize it into an explicit operation. RequiredPriority remains nullable: explicit operations expose the engine value; the convenience overload exposes only movement, so priority is unavailable (null), as clarified in the proposal. Ordinary transfers retain Left/SHIFT and their current automatic/direct priority.

P1.5 -> proposal Transfer result and Validation requirements -> validity, exact settings/dispatch, requested versus actual movement, deferred priority and original exception -> InventoryTransferExecutorTests uses Moq for call-boundary observations and existing real engine fixtures for zero/partial movement and a callback failure after mutation. Existing full-operation suites remain unchanged.

Final verification through phase1_tests against the implementation working tree based on cb58a0278563157353efb2af56af082b1ed9c0e7:

```powershell
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter FullyQualifiedName~InventoryTransferExecutorTests --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests/VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests.csproj --no-restore --filter Category=Integration --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter FullyQualifiedName~AutoStash --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter Category=Unit --verbosity minimal
```

Boundary 12, Integration 1, AutoStash 369 and Unit 565 passed; zero failures/skips/exclusions. All commands were sequential; the dedicated integration host exited before fresh focused and Unit hosts. An initial integration filter against the unit project matched no tests and was discarded, not counted as verification. The boundary build emitted 34 existing warnings (7 production, 27 tests), none from the new files; subsequent incremental runs emitted none. Sandbox artifact-write restrictions were recovered through approved escalation. No runtime game/server/client launch or additional tooling was required.

Second review: re-read the complete controlling proposal and selected/inherited plan requirements; compared both loop diffs against their original engine calls and AutoStashMutationState boundaries; checked new contracts/tests for settings, dispatch and assertion sensitivity. No source selection, policy, lifecycle, networking, interaction or alloy changes were introduced. Independent completion audit by phase1_audit directly consulted the controlling plan/proposal, implementation, engine overloads, regression fixtures and recorded verification. Verdict: P1.1–P1.5 and completion fully satisfied; no confirmed issues or evidence gaps. Markers were checked only after this verdict. Later architecture work and live acceptance remain separate.

## Phase 2: Separate target policies and the incremental cursor

Outcome: Planning selects one live instruction and advances on every outcome while existing owners retain lifecycle.
Dependencies: Phase 1.
Sources: Proposal Policy contracts and Incremental planning and execution; current transfer loops, edge/retry/preferred-slot tests and bloomery execution/boundary tests.

☑ P2.1 Introduce Planning policy contracts for source eligibility, ordered source passes, destination routing and live quantity limits. Implement matching-content, first-type crate and bloomery policies without moving authorization or presentation.
☑ P2.2 Capture initial matching types/first crate type once per operation. Preserve empty-target rejection, collectible-code eligibility versus engine attribute compatibility, backpack-before-hotbar and source-slot order, and missing source inventories.
☑ P2.3 Express bloomery backpack ore → backpack fuel → hotbar ore → hotbar fuel, burning/output rejection, classification/ratios, mandatory ore/fuel indices and limits recalculated from actual live contents. Never route overflow elsewhere.
☑ P2.4 Implement the operation cursor and AutoStashPlanner around the existing source-by-source control flow, retaining engine suitability for ordinary selection. Planning performs no mutation and does not retain instructions beyond immediate execution.
☑ P2.5 Preserve automatic-before-direct deferral, every live eligible empty/same-item direct destination, automatically excluded alternatives and exclusion after rejection. Reconsider rejections only after positive movement; aggregate exhausted source failures without stale failure after that source is completely moved. Prove normal bounded termination with existing guarded retry tests.
☑ P2.6 Resolve and implement the documented preferred-slot compatibility representation before moving its tests; record the choice here. Keep required-slot and preferred-fallback routing distinct. Separate removal/behavior change requires explicit authorization.
☑ P2.7 Feed actual executor outcomes into the cursor after each instruction. Revalidate target-specific live limits immediately before execution and observe state changes during opening/callbacks without reservations or a copied inventory model.
☑ P2.8 Route both existing loop owners through the planner/cursor, retaining current finalization ownership until adapters are available. Remove superseded selection loops in the same change; do not leave two active algorithms.
☑ Completion: Order-sensitive, capacity, attribute, suitability and exhaustive retry regressions pass; focused/Unit suites and independent audit confirm planner purity and finite progress without policy changes.

### Implementation notes and evidence

Implementation starts at 56a1de4b480d3e4f37811fe9f76739f0b7576154 (completed concrete transfer executor). Every item inherits the complete proposal and Scope and execution rules above; no controlling reference was unavailable.

P2.1–P2.3 -> proposal Policy contracts and Data contracts -> snapshot matching codes/first crate type, engine-owned attribute compatibility, ordered sources, bloomery mandatory indices and live allowances -> Planning/AutoStashPolicy, MatchingContentsPolicy, CratePolicy, BloomeryPolicy and AutoStashSourcePass. Block/attached entry points capture matching types before execution; bloomery retains its initial burning/output gate and rechecks CanAdd/classification and quantities for each source/pass. Client-only gates remain in interaction eligibility; extracted classification/capacity helpers are shared without migrating assessment ownership.

P2.4–P2.5/P2.7–P2.8 -> proposal Incremental planning and execution, Target lifecycle and partial failure, Source layout and dependencies -> pure immediate instruction selection, actual-outcome cursor advancement, source-local bounded automatic/direct selection, live limits and unchanged finalization -> AutoStashPlanner/AutoStashCursor and the two original operation owners. GetBestSuitedSlot and engine eligibility remain authoritative; old selection and bloomery source loops were removed. Mutation accounting, persistence/cleanup, audits and owner-only failure chat stay in their original owners. Instructions are executed immediately; quantities are read from current state, including after suitability selection, and subsequent moves observe engine callbacks.

P2.6 -> proposal Starting architecture preferred-slot characterization and Policy contracts required routing -> preserve compatibility without a gameplay change -> PreferredSlotCompatibility names the existing exclusive valid-index preflight and execution preference with fallback. Invalid indices use ordinary selection. Mandatory bloomery routing does not use that boundary and attempts each source only once per pass. Existing preferred-slot tests continue through a predicate/preference-to-policy compatibility overload; production callers use concrete policies. Removing that overload belongs to later cleanup after consumer migration.

Boundary evidence: eight AutoStashPlannerTests cases protect read-only probing/selection, initial type snapshots, first-type/empty crates, missing sources, one outstanding instruction, actual/live capacity advancement and bloomery fuel based on actual ore. Existing edge/retry/preferred/bloomery/failure/persistence suites remain unchanged and provide full-operation conservation, ordering, restrictions, bounded-attempt and lifecycle evidence. First review and a separate second review compared extracted rules and both owner diffs with the original loops and full controlling proposal; no separate audit/verification files were created.

Final verification through phase2_tests against this implementation working tree:

```powershell
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests/VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests.csproj --no-restore --filter 'Category=Integration' --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter 'FullyQualifiedName~AutoStash' --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-build --no-restore --filter 'Category=Unit' --verbosity minimal
```

Integration 1, AutoStash 377 and Unit 573 passed, zero failures/skips/exclusions; all processes exited 0 with no warnings on these final incremental runs. The dedicated integration host exited before each fresh focused/Unit host. Initial compilation failures (ambiguous System.Func and FirstNonEmptySlot ownership) were corrected; a Unit run using the preceding binary while compilation failed was discarded as stale. The successful rebuilt focused run before final acceptance emitted only 34 existing warnings (7 production, 27 tests); new nullable warnings were fixed. No game/client/server launch or new tooling occurred. Independent completion audit by phase2_audit directly consulted the full controlling plan/proposal, extracted policies/planner/cursor, owner diffs, engine behavior and regression evidence. P2.1–P2.8 and completion are fully satisfied with no required gaps. The stale proposal-heading reference was corrected and independently rechecked; markers were checked only after the final clean verdict.

## Phase 3: Make target lifecycle ownership explicit

Outcome: Target adapters own inventory resolution, acquisition, persistence and cleanup for every target.
Dependencies: Phase 2.
Sources: Proposal Attached bags and Target lifecycle and partial failure; existing target entry points, AutoStashMutationState and lifecycle/finalization/persistence tests.

☑ P3.1 Add Targets adapters for ordinary containers/crates, bloomeries and attached bags using their owning engine APIs. Resolve target availability and read-only contents separately from mutation/session acquisition.
☑ P3.2 Acquire ordinary/workspace sessions only after plausible work; close only sessions acquired by this operation. Keep temporary bag inventories session-free and bloomery lifecycle unchanged. Observe resulting live contents after opening.
☑ P3.3 Preserve vanilla workspace refresh/wrapper identity, distinct IHeldBag loading, attachment validation and load-failure no-op effects. Preserve Store for live content slots, attachment dirty marking and actual owner storage, including existing workspace callbacks.
☑ P3.4 Move target finalization into adapters using the established actual/uncertain mutation accounting. Persist or synchronize before owned close; nested cleanup must run despite persistence/reporting failures. Ordinary completed zero moves do not finalize.
☑ P3.5 Preserve original exception identity/stack and report secondary persistence/cleanup failures without replacing it. When no earlier exception exists, propagate the first failure. Do not claim rollback or successful persistence when storage itself throws.
☑ P3.6 Integrate adapters into the existing operation owner without adding a second mutation tracker. Replace session-management booleans and target finalizer callbacks at that boundary with explicit lifecycle ownership.
☑ P3.7 Run acquired/caller-owned, after-earlier-move, after-unreturned-mutation, storage/cleanup failure, zero/partial and bag/owner reload regressions for both bag implementations and actual initialized crate inventories.
☑ Completion: All targets finalize and release through adapters, persistence round trips and failure assertions pass, and focused/Unit verification plus independent audit establish lifecycle ordering/ownership.

### Implementation notes and evidence

Implementation starts at 180cac131fdb53a365e46effc901161681bebb63. Every item inherits the complete proposal and Scope and execution rules; the attached-bag and Target lifecycle and partial failure sections govern acquisition, actual/uncertain mutation, persistence ordering and exceptions. Current owners, AutoStashMutationState and full lifecycle/finalization/bag regression sources were consulted before extraction; no controlling reference was unavailable.

P3.1–P3.3 -> proposal Attached bags, Target lifecycle and partial failure, Source layout and dependencies -> owning engine APIs, read-only resolution, deferred acquisition and stable slot/session identity -> Targets/AutoStashTarget, InventoryAutoStashTarget, ContainerAutoStashTarget, BloomeryAutoStashTarget, AttachedBagAutoStashTarget and AutoStashInventorySession. Ordinary/crate inventories are captured from their owners; bloomery resolution retains burning/output rejection and has no session. Attached resolution validates the slot, bag and positive capacity and reads persisted contents without a workspace. Candidate detection precedes bag execution loading; actual capacity is checked before acquiring ordinary/workspace sessions. Distinct bags use session-free temporary inventories. Vanilla loading retains workspace callbacks and refreshes existing wrapper slots in place; a failed load performs no transfer/session/persistence. Planning reads live post-open state.

P3.4–P3.6 -> proposal Target lifecycle and partial failure -> finalize actual or uncertain changes before release, nested cleanup despite storage/reporting failures, original exception identity/stack, no second tracker -> existing AutoStashMutationState now calls adapter FinalizeChanges/Release directly. Container adapters mark their block entity dirty; bloomery preserves MarkDirty(true); bag adapters Store live slots, mark the attachment dirty and store its owner before workspace close. Ordinary completed zero moves do not finalize. Engine-attempt uncertainty and actual movement tracking remain unchanged. Session-management flags and target-finalizer callbacks were removed from the operation boundary; predicate/preference compatibility remains separate. Bare inventory and finalization tests now use adapters and Moq observations; DynamicProxyGenAssembly2 receives internal access so the adapter types remain internal mod implementation details.

P3.7 -> proposal Validation requirements and established regression contract -> acquired/caller-owned sessions, interruption before/after mutation, live opening state, storage/cleanup/reporting failures, no-op/partial transfers and independent persistence reloads -> unchanged full-operation AutoStashLifecycleTests, BloomeryFailureTests, AttachedContainerFailureTests and EntityAttachedContainerTransfer/PersistenceTests, including initialized crate inventories and both bag implementations. Migrated AutoStashFinalizationTests retain their original assertions and add two broken-logger variants plus an original engine stack assertion. Six AutoStashTargetTests cases cover read-only attached resolution, no-candidate workspace avoidance and exact session release ownership.

Final verification through phase2_tests against the implementation working tree, sequential commands with dedicated integration-host termination before fresh focused/Unit hosts:

```powershell
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests/VanillaExpanded.IntegrationTests.csproj --no-restore --filter 'Category=Integration' --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter 'FullyQualifiedName~AutoStash' --verbosity minimal
dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-build --no-restore --filter 'Category=Unit' --verbosity minimal
```

Integration 1, AutoStash 385 and Unit 581 passed, zero failures/skips/exclusions; every process exited 0. The final integration build emitted 27 existing test warnings, none new; subsequent runs emitted none. Earlier compilation also retained the seven existing production warnings. Initial seven Moq access failures were resolved with internal proxy access; Unit was withheld until the rebuilt focused suite passed. Fresh installed metadata: API/Lib/SurvivalMod/Essentials assemblies all 1.22.7.0, API file 1.22.0, others 1.22.7. No game/client/server launch, tooling installation or separate audit/verification files occurred.

Second review re-read the controlling proposal/plan and checked owner/adapter/tracker diffs, deferred bag loading, exact engine identities/callbacks, finalization ordering, conservative uncertainty and original/secondary exception paths. No planner policy, network, interaction timing or alloy behavior was changed. Storage failure still provides no rollback or durability guarantee. Independent completion audit by phase2_audit directly consulted the controlling plan/proposal, adapter/owner/tracker implementation, regression fixtures and final verification evidence. Verdict: P3.1–P3.7 and completion fully satisfied, with no remaining required implementation or evidence gaps. Task markers were checked only after this clean verdict. Shared operation coordination, assessment and live acceptance remain separate.

## Phase 4: Introduce shared operation coordination

Outcome: All server target paths use one planner/executor coordinator and a truthful operation result.
Dependencies: Phases 2–3.
Sources: Proposal Responsibilities, Operation result, Incremental planning and execution and Scope; server dispatch and direct-feedback regressions.

☑ P4.1 Add AutoStashService coordinating source resolution/type snapshots, plausible-work probe, adapter acquisition, live planning, immediate execution, cursor advancement and finalization. Keep requests synchronous and best-effort.
☑ P4.2 Add AutoStashResult with actual moved total and unavailable/no-candidate/no-destination/success distinctions, including partial success and a separate exhausted-direct indicator. Throwing operations preserve exceptions; they are not converted into completed results.
☑ P4.3 Preserve existing per-move/aggregate audit behavior and one owning-player general-chat CommandError after normal finalization/owned cleanup for exhausted direct attempts, including partial movement. Resolve ownership by inventory-manager identity; ordinary full/no-work remains silent.
☑ P4.4 Delegate generic, crate, bloomery and attached server entry points to the shared service. Preserve registered request handlers, permission/reachability checks, exact six-block entity distance, current-state resolution and packet contracts.
☑ P4.5 Retain compatibility wrappers only where entry signatures provide a real boundary; remove obsolete AutoStashTransferService orchestration after its callers migrate. Update internal tests to exercise the owning service without losing full-operation/dispatch coverage.
☑ P4.6 Verify changed-target type/capacity after client assessment, denied/missing targets, target-specific dispatch outcomes and all lifecycle/retry regressions. Run isolated Harmony integration before fresh focused/Unit hosts because entry wiring changed.
☑ Completion: Every target shares coordinator/executor, existing server boundaries remain thin and authoritative, all required suites pass, and independent audit confirms truthful results/feedback and no duplicated operation algorithm.

### Implementation notes and evidence

Implementation starts at b530316deba9cdf85226cdf044190ed2e198fd1a. P4.1–P4.6 inherit the complete proposal and Scope and execution rules; the Responsibilities, Operation result, Incremental planning and execution, Target lifecycle and partial failure, Scope and Validation requirements sections govern this extraction. Current server handlers and ServerBlockRequestTests/ServerEntityRequestTests were consulted directly, including changed-type/capacity, permission, missing-target and six-block boundary cases. No controlling source was unavailable.

P4.1/P4.4/P4.5 -> proposal Responsibilities, Policy contracts, Source layout and dependencies -> service-owned source resolution/type snapshots, adapter preparation/acquisition, immediate live planning/execution and finalization -> AutoStashService target operations and common Execute loop. The separate bloomery operation loop and obsolete AutoStashTransferService are removed; existing Boolean block/attached entry signatures remain meaningful adapters. Internal callers construct concrete policies, retaining preferred-slot compatibility without a predicate wrapper. Server authorization, packets, interaction and alloy code retain their ownership.

P4.2/P4.3 -> proposal Operation result, Incremental planning and execution and Target lifecycle and partial failure -> actual movement, independent exhausted-direct failure, exact audit templates, owner-only feedback after cleanup and exception propagation -> AutoStashResult/AutoStashOutcome, AutoStashAuditContext and the common service. Candidate membership is now distinct from destination capacity: ordinary policy membership uses its existing eligibility; bloomery candidate detection classifies inputs while execution still uses the original engine CanAdd and live limits. A full bloomery with valid input reports NoDestination. Failed bag preparation reports Unavailable. This reporting distinction does not change engine execution eligibility or routing.

P4.6 -> proposal Validation requirements and established regression contract -> AutoStashServiceTests cover completed outcome distinctions, exact partial totals, zero and positive movement alongside direct failure, ore/fuel totals and exact bloomery audit templates, and independent persistence of both attached-bag paths. Existing full-operation, dispatch, lifecycle and guarded retry tests remain intact; internal service tests migrated without weakening their inventory or exception assertions.

